  "verbalization": "Lancifodilactone N is shown to interact with Caspase-5, a key protease involved in apoptosis and cellular death. Caspase-5 is further targeted by two distinct compounds: (2R,3S,4R,5R,6S)-2-[2-(3,4-dihydroxy-5-methoxyphenyl)-7-hydroxy-3-[(2R,3S,4R,5R,6S)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxychromenylium-5-yl]oxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxane-3,4,5-triol and (1R,4Ar,7R)-7-Ethenyl-1,4A,7-Trimethyl-3,4,4B,5,6,9,10,10A-Octahydro-2H-Phenanthrene-1-Carboxylic Acid. Both of these compounds are known to target prostate cells, suggesting a potential mechanism by which Lancifodilactone N may exert its effects on prostate cells. The evidence suggests that Lancifodilactone N may act through Caspase-5 to induce apoptosis in prostate cells, potentially offering a therapeutic approach for prostate-related conditions. The strength of the evidence is moderate, as the scores are relatively low, but the consistent targeting of Caspase-5 and the subsequent effect on prostate cells across multiple pathways supports a plausible mechanism.",
